Neural correlates of prediction errors of perceived exertion: an fMRI dataset

Raw 3T MRI data (T1-weighted anatomical images and task fMRI) from 79 healthy adults who imagined running along city and forest trails with lower-than-expected (positive PEexert) or higher-than-expected (negative PEexert) physical effort. Data were collected in November 2025 on a 3T Siemens MAGNETOM Prisma Fit at the Ghent Institute for Functional and Metabolic Imaging (GIfMI), Ghent University Hospital, supported by Ghent University (grant D/01357/02). 82 participants were scanned in total; 3 did not consent to public data sharing and are omitted from this repository, leaving 79 participants in this dataset.

Anatomical: MPRAGE, 1 mm isotropic. Functional: EPI, TR = 1.07 s, 2.5 mm isotropic, 590 volumes.
Field map: multi-echo gradient echo (TR = 588 ms, TE1 = 4.92 ms, TE2 = 7.38 ms, flip angle = 60 degrees), two magnitude images and one phase-difference image per participant.
Events: onsets and durations in seconds relative to the first fMRI volume (see task-imagine_events.json).

Of the 82 scanned participants, 79 were included in the study analyses (including the 3 participants who did not consent to public data sharing), while 3 participants in this dataset (sub-009, sub-028, sub-073) were excluded during data analysis because event timing could not be verified (optimal onset shifts of +8 to +13.5 s, versus -2 to +1.5 s in the included participants). Consequently, 76 of the 79 participants shared here were part of the analysed sample. participants.tsv shows, for each participant in this dataset, whether the person was included in the analyses (included_in_analysis, exclusion_reason). The task is described in task-imagine_bold.json.

Participant labels follow the original numbering (sub-032 corresponds to the original participant 32). Anatomical images were defaced and identifying information was removed from the metadata.

